# UX-SPEC.md §Board · KEYMAP.md §Workspace (Native mode) and §Board and card detail ·
# BOARD.md §8 — `ctrl-s b` opens this worktree's own `fleet://board` tab, the pane is the Hub's
# board with the Hub's board keys, and the Hub still shows the *context* board afterwards.
#
# The `board` preset seeds two boards under one context (§4): the context board, whose cards are
# numbered `FLT-…`, and `acme/api#feature`'s own, numbered `FEA-…` from the prefix the daemon
# derives from the slug. Their card sets are disjoint, so `lists["board.cards"]` alone says which
# of the two a surface is drawing — which is what makes the last third of this file the roadmap's
# context-board-lookup regression check rather than a second copy of `board/columns.scenario`.
#
# A tab row carries its name and its `native`/`pty` badge, never its command, so `board` here is
# the name the app gives the reserved `fleet://board` command, and the tab is matched by command
# only inside the app (`screens/workspace/actions.rs`). The tab is created on demand and is not in
# config.json's `windows[]`: it is never one of the three tabs a session starts with, and a
# sleep/wake does not bring it back.
#
# Headless: virtual-lane only, because of its `shot`. The keyboard does reach the focused view in
# the headless lane (§7), so everything else here would run there.
fixture: board
await idle 20000
assert screen == Hub && hub_tab == Worktrees

# Open the only worktree's session. Its three tabs are config.json's default windows, and there
# is no fourth: nothing has asked for the board tab yet.
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000
await lists.tabs.rows[2] exists 20000
assert lists.tabs.rows[3] absent && mode == Terminal

# `ctrl-s b` asks fleetd for the tab and selects the reply.
key ctrl-s b
await lists.tabs.rows[3].label == "board" 20000
assert lists.tabs.rows[3].badges[0] == native
await lists.tabs.selected.label == "board" 10000

# The tab draws the board itself rather than handing its keys to an embedded view, so the chain
# names the surface a third word deep and the cursor opens on the first card of the first column.
await focused == "board.column[0].card[0]" 20000
# The mode word is awaited rather than asserted (§9.3): the tab is created and then selected, and
# the select mutation is still in flight when the pane first draws — `select_terminal` cannot read
# the kind of a terminal the mirror has not received yet, so the word is `Terminal` for the one
# frame between the pane arriving and the snapshot that carries its record.
await mode == Native 10000
assert screen == Workspace
assert key_contexts[0] == "Workspace" && key_contexts[1] == "Native"
assert key_contexts[2] == "Board"

# It is *this worktree's* board: three cards in two columns, numbered from the slug's prefix.
assert lists.board.rows[0].label == "Backlog" && lists.board.rows[0].badges[0] == "2"
assert lists.board.rows[1].label == "Todo" && lists.board.rows[1].badges[0] == "1"
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "FEA-1"
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].label == "Open the tab with ctrl-s b"
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[1].badges[0] == "FEA-2"

# `]` is a board key and it reaches the pane: the card changes column, both counts move by one,
# and the cursor follows the card into its new column.
key ]
await lists.board.rows[0].badges[0] == "1" 10000
assert lists.board.rows[1].badges[0] == "2"
assert lists.board.selected.label == "Todo" && focused == "board.column[1].card[1]"
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[1].badges[0] == "FEA-1"
shot board-tab

# Pressing it again is idempotent: the tab is found by its command and only selected, so no
# fourth tab is created and the cursor has not moved.
key ctrl-s b
await idle 10000
assert lists.tabs.rows[4] absent && lists.tabs.selected.label == "board"
assert focused == "board.column[1].card[1]" && key_contexts[2] == "Board"

# `ctrl-s 1` leaves the pane for the first PTY, and the board word leaves the chain with it.
key ctrl-s 1
await lists.tabs.selected.id == 1 10000
assert mode == Terminal && key_contexts[1] == "Terminal" && key_contexts[2] absent

# `ctrl-s s` back to the Hub, then `g b` for its board — which is the context board. A worktree
# board must never be what a context-board lookup finds, and the `]` above must not have touched
# it: five `FLT-…` cards, still 2 / 2 / 1 across the first three columns.
key ctrl-s s
await screen == Hub 10000
key g b
await hub_tab == Board 10000
await lists["board.cards"].rows[0].badges[0] == "FLT-1" 20000
assert lists["board.cards"].rows[0].label == "Photograph the daemon-down screen"
assert lists.board.rows[0].badges[0] == "2" && lists.board.rows[1].badges[0] == "2"
assert lists.board.rows[2].badges[0] == "1" && lists.board.rows[3].badges[0] == "0"
assert focused == "board.column[0].card[0]" && key_contexts[1] == "Board"
dump context-board
quit
